---
import Lab from '../layouts/Lab.astro';

const work = [
  ['AI integration', 'an agent that edits the model as a program: typed tools over the document, a rebuild and a check after every edit, and a verdict from the Sovware kernel before anything is called done', 'design'],
  ['Sketcher', 'a sketch workspace that stays fluid under constraints: fewer modes, solver feedback you can read, dimensions you type', 'planned'],
  ['Part design', 'the feature history as the source of truth, with named parameters, equations and clear errors when a rebuild fails', 'planned'],
  ['Assemblies', 'one assembly workbench, joints and constraints that survive edits, bills of materials that are right', 'planned'],
  ['PCB and KiCad', 'boards and enclosures together: KiCad in, outlines and keep-outs out, and a report the circuits gate can verify', 'planned'],
  ['Drafting', '2D drawings from the model that stay in step with it: views, sections, dimensions, title blocks', 'planned'],
  ['Architecture and BIM', 'walls, openings, levels and IFC, built on the same history and the same checks', 'planned'],
];

const status = [
  ['Fork', 'starting', 'FreeCAD, with its OpenCASCADE kernel and workbenches; the fork keeps file compatibility'],
  ['Platforms', 'macOS first', 'Linux and Windows follow FreeCAD'],
  ['License', 'LGPL-2.1+', 'FreeCAD\'s licence carries over to the fork; see the repository'],
  ['Research preview', 'archived', 'the macOS preview with its own exact kernel (NURBS, Metal, feature IR) set the bar and stays in the history'],
  ['Kernel gate', 'draft', 'Nightfall is the first CAD domain for the Sovware verifier kernel'],
];
---

<Lab
  path="/"
  current="overview"
  og="/og/nightfall.png"
  description="Nightfall is AI-native CAD: a fork of FreeCAD that makes the agent a first-class user of the sketcher, part design, assemblies, PCB and KiCad, drafting and BIM, with every edit rebuilt and checked."
>
  <h1>nightfall</h1>
  <p class="lede">AI-native CAD. A fork of FreeCAD where an agent edits the model as a program, and every edit is rebuilt and checked.</p>

  <div class="actions">
    <a class="button button--primary" href="https://github.com/wckdboy/nightfall" rel="noopener">Source</a>
    <a class="text-link" href="/#work">The work</a>
    <a class="text-link" href="/brand/">Brand</a>
  </div>

  <dl class="facts">
    <div><dt>Base</dt><dd>FreeCAD</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Kernel</dt><dd>OpenCASCADE</dd></div>
    <div><dt>State</dt><dd>forking</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Telemetry</dt><dd>none</dd></div>
  </dl>

  <section id="why">
    <h2>Why a fork</h2>
    <p>FreeCAD is the open parametric CAD: an exact kernel, a sketcher, part design, assemblies, drafting, architecture and a long memory of real parts. What it lacks is a way for an agent to work in it that is more than pasting Python. Nightfall starts from FreeCAD instead of from a kernel of its own, and spends the effort on the parts that make an AI a useful co-worker: the model as a program it can read and edit, a rebuild and a check after every edit, and workspaces that stay fluid when a constraint fights back.</p>
    <p>The rules do not change. Math is the source of truth and meshes are derived. Every part is a program: a feature list with named parameters, geometry as a cache. The agent edits the program, never the geometry. Built for making real things: millimetres, explicit tolerances, STEP, 3MF and SVG out.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="work">
    <h2>The work</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>What changes</th><th>State</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {work.map(([area, what, state]) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{area}</strong></td><td>{what}</td><td class="small">{state}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small">Order of work: the fork and its build, then AI integration and the sketcher, then part design and assemblies. PCB, drafting and BIM follow, each as a domain the kernel can gate.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="status">
    <h2>Status</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          {status.map(([part, state, note]) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{part}</strong></td><td>{state}</td><td class="small">{note}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small">Nothing on this page is shipping yet. The state column says what exists; the rest is the plan.</p>
  </section>
</Lab>
